{
  "action": "tool",
  "tool": "retrieve_history",
  "args": {
    "research_concepts": [
      "structural roles (organizer, shaper, terminal)",
      "lineup composition",
      "player talent quantification",
      "separability of player attributes/roles",
      "within-team variation in lineup construction"
    ],
    "methodological_questions": [
      "How to define and assign structural roles to players?",
      "Methods for controlling for player talent when assessing lineup effects.",
      "Statistical approaches to demonstrate separability of lineup component effects from individual player attributes (e.g., talent, counts).",
      "Challenges and risks in analyzing lineup performance and structural composition."
    ],
    "query": "Research on NBA structural roles (organizer, shaper, terminal) in lineup composition, methods for controlling player talent, and testing separability of role effects from talent/player counts, focusing on within-team variation."
  }
}

TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "structural roles (organizer, shaper, terminal)",
    "lineup composition",
    "player talent quantification",
    "separability of player attributes/roles",
    "within-team variation in lineup construction"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to define and assign structural roles to players?",
    "Methods for controlling for player talent when assessing lineup effects.",
    "Statistical approaches to demonstrate separability of lineup component effects from individual player attributes (e.g., talent, counts).",
    "Challenges and risks in analyzing lineup performance and structural composition."
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "No historical evidence was available to establish any findings or limitations for this research question."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The available data, including play-by-play and derived play-type possession records, offers sufficient granularity to attempt to define structural roles (organizer, shaper, terminal) based on player actions (initiator, play type).",
    "Player talent can be estimated using various per-possession or per-minute metrics derivable from the box-score and play-by-play data, such as points, assists, rebounds, usage, and efficiency ratings.",
    "The question involves both descriptive (variation) and inferential (separability, control behavior) components, requiring distinct analytical approaches.",
    "The large number of possessions (420,993) and team-seasons (90) provides a strong foundation for statistical analysis, although sparsity for specific lineup configurations remains a potential issue."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The precise methodology for empirically defining and assigning 'organizer', 'shaper', and 'terminal' roles from the available play-type data is not specified and requires careful development and validation.",
    "The specific metrics and methods for quantifying 'player talent' and 'player counts' for control purposes need to be determined.",
    "The statistical approach to demonstrate 'separability' of lineup coverage from talent/counts is not detailed and will be critical to the research design.",
    "The expected magnitude of within-team variation in lineup coverage is unknown prior to analysis."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No historical evidence was retrieved, so it did not change the decision."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.8,
    "blocking_constraints": []
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No prior work on this specific research question was retrieved, suggesting it has not been directly answered by previous studies in this project."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Defining 'organizer, shaper, terminal' roles empirically from play-type data is complex and subjective. The reliability and validity of these role assignments will be critical and must be thoroughly tested. Different definitions could lead to different conclusions."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Player talent is highly confounded with structural roles. Star players often naturally take on primary organizer or shaper roles. Separating the marginal effect of the 'role being filled' from the 'effect of the talented player filling it' is a significant challenge. Lineup decisions are not random; coaches deploy players in roles where they excel."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "While overall possessions are high, specific combinations of lineup structural coverage (e.g., exact counts of O, S, T players) and talent levels might have limited minutes/possessions, leading to low statistical power for certain comparisons or requiring aggregation that might obscure nuance."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "If multiple definitions of roles, talent controls, or interaction terms are explored to achieve 'separability' or demonstrate 'talent control behaves', there is an increased risk of false positives or finding spurious relationships due to repeated hypothesis testing."
    },
    {
      "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
      "text": "Care must be taken to ensure that player role assignments or talent measures do not incorporate information from future possessions or outcomes, which could artificially inflate perceived effects. All measures should be contemporaneous or lagged relative to the lineup being evaluated."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.4,
    "rejected": 0.2,
    "untestable": 0.1,
    "artifact": 0.1,
    "uncertain": 0.2
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "1. Descriptive: The distribution and frequency of distinct lineup structural coverage configurations (e.g., counts of players identified as 'organizer', 'shaper', 'terminal') within each team-season. 2. Inferential (Separability): The marginal effect of a lineup meeting the 'coverage' criteria (>=1 organizer, >=1 shaper, >=1 terminal) on offensive/defensive efficiency (e.g., points per possession, effective field goal percentage), after controlling for individual player talent (e.g., EPM, RAPTOR, or similar per-possession/per-minute metrics) and simple player counts (e.g., number of guards, forwards, centers). 3. Methodological (Talent Control Behavior): The correlation between the controlled talent metrics and individual player contribution, and the robustness of the control method in isolating structural effects from player-specific effects, potentially through residual analysis or placebo tests.",
    "unit": "Possession. Each possession will be associated with the five players on the court, their assigned structural roles, their individual talent metrics, and the outcome of the possession.",
    "sample": "All 420,993 possessions from NBA 2022-23 to 2025-26 regular seasons and playoffs. Data from 2025-26 will be used as available and noted if incomplete.",
    "power": "Power analysis will be conducted after initial data exploration and role definition to estimate the minimum detectable effect size for the separability claim. Given 420,993 possessions, reasonable power is expected for detecting moderate effects, but specific lineup combinations may suffer from sparsity. Stratified sampling or weighting might be considered if certain configurations are too rare.",
    "controls": "Individual player talent will be controlled using a robust, pre-defined, composite per-possession efficiency metric (e.g., derived from points, assists, turnovers, rebounds, defensive stops) for each player in the lineup. Additionally, simple player counts (e.g., number of guards, forwards, centers, or overall athleticism proxies) will be controlled. Player roles (organizer, shaper, terminal) will be assigned based on their observed play-type initiation rates and types within a rolling window or across a season, avoiding lookahead bias. The control methodology should involve player-level fixed effects or hierarchical models to account for player heterogeneity.",
    "falsification": "The 'talent control behaves' aspect will be tested by examining if the controlled talent metrics correlate with expected individual player contributions and if the structural effect persists after rigorous talent control. A falsification test could involve demonstrating that randomly assigned 'structural roles' (while keeping talent constant) do not yield significant effects, or that the structural effect diminishes when talent control is insufficient. Another test could be to show that the structural effect is not merely a proxy for the sum of individual player talent.",
    "multiplicity": "A clear hierarchy of hypotheses will be established. The primary hypothesis is the separability claim. Exploratory analyses for role definition and talent control validation will be clearly distinguished from confirmatory tests. If multiple role definitions or talent control methods are explored, p-value adjustments (e.g., Bonferroni, FDR) will be considered for the final separability claim, or a single, pre-specified method will be chosen based on pilot validation.",
    "decision_rule": "The research question will be considered 'supported' if: 1) Significant within-team variation in lineup structural coverage is demonstrated. 2) A statistically significant marginal effect of lineup structural coverage (e.g., >=1 O, S, T) on team efficiency is observed, *after* controlling for individual player talent and simple player counts. 3) The chosen talent control method is shown to 'behave' as expected (e.g., talent metrics correlate with performance, and the control effectively isolates structural effects without over-explaining the variance). A p-value threshold of 0.05 will be used for statistical significance.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25 complete regular season and playoffs. NBA 2025-26 regular season complete as of 2026-04-13 and playoffs complete as of 2026-06-21. All data sourced from NBA stats API and derived play-type possession records."
  }
}

